<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Feb 19, 2023">
<CPscript title="Owl Play" level="min" >
<description>The CirclePack 'Owl' is useful for displaying CirclePack manipulations. Run through this script once by just clicking 'Next'. Then open with the "Script" button and read as you go through again. Try some of the options provided. In euclidean and hyperbolic geometry, for example, you can use the mouse and keys to adjust radii and repack.</description>
<text> The 'Owl' packing is a simple circle packing (just 80 circles) which is convenient for illustrations --- in particular, the 'eyes', colored blue, provide a reference the observer can follow in various manipulations. Here we work first in euclidean geometry, then in hyperbolic geometry (the unit disc), and finally in spherical geometry. </text>
<Section title="Euclidean Owl">
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" tooltip="Start over with a fresh 'owl'" iconname="apollon.png">act 0;cleanse;cleanse -p1;cleanse -p2;infile_read Owl.p;color -c 20 21 22;color -c 180 b;disp -wr; </cmd>
<text> In each geometry, the tools 'b', 'k', and 'f' (see their icons donw in the "Tools" Section) will display, respectively, the boundary circles (in red), the 'complex' K of edges that specify the tangencies that must be maintained, and 'carrier' (triangulation) of faces formed by triples of circles. </text>
<text> In euclidean and hyperbolic geometry, you can place the cursor on a boundary circle and press 'i' (increase) or 'd' (decrease) to adjust its radius. A computation --- discussed in the Chapter --- adjusts the packing to incorporate this new boundary data. You might try, for example, to find the boundary circle(s) whose adjustments have the greatest impact on the overall packing. </text>
<cmd iconname="klines.png">[b] </cmd>
<cmd iconname="kghostview.png">[k] </cmd>
<cmd iconname="amarok.png">[f] </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Hyperbolic Owl">
<text> The next command moves the owl to hyperbolic geometry. The command 'I' (capital eye) increments all boundary radii at once. (Go back to the euclidean setting and try the same thing. What do you observe?)

Note that the "lines" (geodesics) in hyperbolic geometry are arcs lying in circles which are orthogonal to the unit circle. If you press 'I' a few times you will see some boundary circles approaching the unit circle. In the limit these are called "horocycles" and serve as circles of infinite hyperbolic radius; press 'k' and you will see that their centers are on the unit circle. </text>
<cmd tooltip="Move to 'hyperbolic' geometry" iconname="icq_dnd.png">act 1;infile_read Owl.p;geom_to_h;color -c 20 21 22;color -c 180 b;disp -wr; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="run.png">[b];[k]; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Spherical Owl">
<text> Finally, we move the owl to the sphere. You can rotate the sphere with the mouse to see other views and use the 'b', 'k', and 'f' keys for display. However, 'i' and 'd' won't work -- the sphere is a tough environment and we have no packing algorithm. </text>
<cmd name="s" mnemonic="s" tooltip="project to the sphere" iconname="network.png">copy 2;act 2;geom_to_s;Disp -w -c -cf 21 22 b; </cmd>
<text> A final note: Invoking the "Advanced" menu item, you see that we have a separate copy of the owl for each of the three geometries. You can choose (by double clicking) which of the packings to make 'active'', which will bring it into the main window where the display options we discussed can be applied. </text>
<cmd iconname="list.png">[b];[k]; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="i" mnemonic="i" tooltip="With cursor on a boundary circle, this will increase its radius" iconname="editdelete.png">set_vlist z #XY;adjust_rad 1.25 vlist;repack;layout;disp -wr; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="d" mnemonic="d" tooltip="With cursor on a boundary circle, this will decrease its radius" iconname="network.png">set_vlist z #XY;adjust_rad .75 vlist;repack;layout;disp -wr; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="b" mnemonic="b" tooltip="Make boundary circles red" iconname="metacontact_online.png">disp -cf b; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="k" mnemonic="k" tooltip="Show the underlying 'complex' of edges and faces" iconname="userconfig.png">disp -f; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="f" mnemonic="f" tooltip="show filled carrier faces" iconname="debugger.png">disp -ff; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="I" mnemonic="I" tooltip="Increment all the boundary radii" iconname="metacontact_offline.png">adjust_rad 1.25 b;repack;layout;disp -wr; </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
<CPdata>
